  "description": "A parametric MIRROR on the six-node spine — Geometry → Input Properties → Math Engine → Output Metrics → 2D Render + 3D Render.\n\nSEGMENT-HOSTED, because a mirror is a wall thing. Drop it on a wall and it takes the wall's direction; \"Height above floor\" lifts it, so the plan shows the thin sliver it actually occupies rather than a rectangle lying on the floor. That sliver is the honest plan symbol — a mirror has almost no footprint.\n\nTHREE SHAPES, TWO GEOMETRIES. Rectangular is a four-bar frame around a glass panel, the same construction the rug's border uses. Round and Oval abandon the quad table for a ring; Round forces equal radii from the smaller dimension while Oval takes width and height as they come, which is the only difference between them.\n\nGLASS AREA is published for whichever shape is active — the rectangle net of its frame, or π·r·r for the ring. That is the figure a glazier quotes against.",
